(Levels of structural organisation) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (LEVELS OF STRUCTURAL ORGANISATION \(sample notes\)) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (The body is organised in a hierarchy:) Tj
T* (1. Chemical level: atoms combine into molecules \(for example, proteins, lipids, nucleic) Tj
T* (acids\).) Tj
T* (2. Cellular level: molecules form organelles and cells, the smallest living units.) Tj
T* (3. Tissue level: groups of similar cells with a common function. The four primary tissue) Tj
T* (types are epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous tissue.) Tj
T* (4. Organ level: two or more tissue types working together \(for example, the stomach\).) Tj
T* (5. Organ system level: organs that cooperate on a common purpose \(for example, the digestive) Tj
T* (system\).) Tj
T* (6. Organismal level: all organ systems together form the whole organism.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (Clinical link: many diseases are understood by asking which level is affected.) Tj
T* (Atherosclerosis, for example, begins at the cellular and tissue level \(endothelial injury,) Tj
T* (lipid deposition\) and becomes an organ-level problem when it narrows coronary arteries.) Tj
